-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/6e2b82e7-65c5-4523-b68b-a16b687f24f7
-- title:
--   Lemma 5 — concentration of $\sum_t(\hat L_t(q_t)-L_t(q_t))$ for predictable comparators
-- statement:
--   Fix integers $d\ge 2$ and $1\le m\le d-1$. Let $T\ge1$ and $\delta\in(0,1)$, let $\eta$ be the learning rate of Algorithm 1 and $\lambda=128\eta d$. Consider a run of Algorithm 1 against an adaptive non-anticipating adversary on a probability space with a filtration $(\mathcal F_t)$, let $\delta_{\mathrm{aux}}\in(0,1)$, and let $(q_t)_{t=1}^T$ be distributions in $\Delta(\mathcal X)$ such that $q_t$ is $\mathcal F_{t-1}$-measurable for every $t$. With
--   $$
--   L_t(q_t)=\mathbb E_{X\sim q_t}[X^\top\ell_t],\qquad \hat L_t(q_t)=\mathbb E_{X\sim q_t}[\hat y_t(X)],\qquad \hat y_t(x)=x^\top\hat\ell_t,
--   $$
--   with probability at least $1-\delta_{\mathrm{aux}}$,
--   $$
--   \Big|\sum_{t=1}^T\big(\hat L_t(q_t)-L_t(q_t)\big)\Big|\le\frac\eta4\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbb E_{X\sim q_t}[c_t^\top X]+\frac{\log(2/\delta_{\mathrm{aux}})}\eta .
--   $$
--
--   In the proof of Theorem 1 it is applied to the constant comparators $q_x=(1-\lambda)\delta_x+\lambda U$, one for each action, and combined by a union bound.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: rounds $t=1,\dots,T$ → rounds indexed $0,\dots,T-1$; reason: Lean convention; same instances
--   - encoding: items $1,\dots,d$ → items $0,\dots,d-1$ (`Fin d`); reason: Lean convention; same instances
--   - encoding: actions $x\in\{0,1\}^d$ with $\|x\|_1=m$ → $m$-element subsets of `Fin d` (`Action d m`) with indicator vector `ind`; reason: the page identifies actions with $m$-sets (p.3); same instances
--   - encoding: "with probability at least $1-\delta$, $A\le B$" → $\mathbb P\{B<A\}\le\delta$, with $\mathbb P$ evaluated as an outer measure (measurability of the event is not assumed); reason: same claim; outer measure agrees with the measure on measurable events
--   - generalization: line 11 runs the ellipsoid procedure of Appendix D.1 for a predetermined number of iterations "with a sufficiently large constant" → $\theta_{t+1}$ is any vector with $\theta_{t+1}-\tilde\theta_{t+1}\in\mathcal D_{\mathrm p}$ satisfying (65) whenever $\mathrm{KL}(U\|p_{\tilde\theta_{t+1}})\le T$; reason: the paper's procedure outputs such a vector (Lemma 8) and the proofs use only (65); the statement specializes to the paper's run
--   - generalization: the filtration generated by the interaction history → any filtration for which $\ell_t$ and $\theta_t$ are $\mathcal F_{t-1}$-measurable, $X_t$ is $\mathcal F_t$-measurable and $X_t$ has conditional law $p_t$ given $\mathcal F_{t-1}$; reason: the history filtration has these properties; the statement specializes to it
-- source:
--   Bacchiocchi–Cesari–Colomboni, An Efficient Minimax-Optimal Algorithm for Adversarial m-Set Bandits, arXiv:2608.12231, Appendix C, Lemma 5, p.27

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MSetBandit_IsAlgRun
import Definitions.Def_MSetBandit_IsDist
import Definitions.Def_MSetBandit_expect
open MeasureTheory Matrix

namespace MSetBandit

/-- **Lemma 5** (Bacchiocchi–Cesari–Colomboni, *An Efficient Minimax-Optimal Algorithm for Adversarial m-Set Bandits*, arXiv:2608.12231, Appendix C, p.27). When running Algorithm 1, let `δ_aux ∈ (0, 1)` and let
`(q_t)` be a sequence of distributions in `∆(X)` such that `q_t` is `𝓕_{t−1}`-measurable for every
round `t`. With `L_t(q_t) := E_{X∼q_t}[X^⊤ℓ_t]` and `L̂_t(q_t) := E_{X∼q_t}[ŷ_t(X)]`,
`ŷ_t(x) = x^⊤ℓ̂_t`, with probability at least `1 − δ_aux`,
`|Σ_{t=1}^T (L̂_t(q_t) − L_t(q_t))| ≤ (η/4) Σ_{t=1}^T E_{X∼q_t}[c_t^⊤X] + log(2/δ_aux)/η`.

Stated for every run of Algorithm 1 (`IsAlgRun`) on a probability space with a filtration, with
`T ≥ 1`, `δ ∈ (0, 1)`, `1 ≤ m ≤ d − 1`; "with probability at least `1 − δ_aux`" is
`P{bound < |Σ_t (L̂_t − L_t)|} ≤ δ_aux`.

**Formalization Note.** Rounds are 0-based: the paper's `𝓕_{t−1}` for round `t` is `𝓕 t` for
round `t < T`. A distribution sequence is a map `q : ℕ → Ω → (Action d m → ℝ)`, each `q t ω` in
`∆(X)` for `t < T`. Natural logarithm. -/
theorem lemma_5 {d m : ℕ} (hm : 1 ≤ m) (hmd : m + 1 ≤ d) {T : ℕ} (hT : 1 ≤ T) {δ : ℝ}
    (hδ0 : 0 < δ) (hδ1 : δ < 1) {Ω : Type*} [mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω)
    [IsProbabilityMeasure P] (𝓕 : Filtration ℕ mΩ) (ℓ θ : ℕ → Ω → Fin d → ℝ)
    (X : ℕ → Ω → Action d m) (hrun : IsAlgRun d m T δ P 𝓕 ℓ θ X) {δaux : ℝ}
    (hδaux0 : 0 < δaux) (hδaux1 : δaux < 1) (q : ℕ → Ω → Action d m → ℝ)
    (hq : ∀ t < T, ∀ ω, IsDist (q t ω)) (hqm : ∀ t < T, Measurable[𝓕 t] (q t)) :
    P {ω | etaAlg d m T δ / 4 * ∑ t ∈ Finset.range T,
            expect (q t ω) (fun x => cvec (wdist (m := m) (θ t ω)) ⬝ᵥ ind x)
          + Real.log (2 / δaux) / etaAlg d m T δ <
        |∑ t ∈ Finset.range T,
          (expect (q t ω) (fun x => ind x ⬝ᵥ lossEst (θ t ω) (X t ω) (ind (X t ω) ⬝ᵥ ℓ t ω)) -
            expect (q t ω) (fun x => ind x ⬝ᵥ ℓ t ω))|} ≤ ENNReal.ofReal δaux := by sorry

end MSetBandit
